Goal

Add a new mandatory Select Difficulty step to Quick Battle between Select Mode and Select Fighter.

The Quick Battle setup flow must become:

Splash → Home → Select Mode → Select Difficulty → Select Fighter → Select Stage → Battle

The player must choose exactly one of:

Easy
Medium
Hard
Brutal

That selected difficulty controls the CPU fighter in Quick Battle.

The CPU must no longer be a passive training dummy in Quick Battle. It must actually fight the player using Alva’s real movement, attacks, Shield, Charge, charged actions, projectiles, jumps, stage movement, etc.

Difficulty must affect the CPU’s reaction speed, perception/reassessment cadence, decision quality, risk assessment, planning, mistakes, defense, aggression, punish ability, and randomness.

Difficulty must NOT give the CPU artificial character-stat advantages.

Practice Ground must remain a training environment and its existing TrainingAIController must remain passive exactly as intended.

Current architecture that must be respected

Alva already has a clean controller boundary:

Fighter.update() asks its controller for a standard FighterInput snapshot.

PlayerController supplies human inputs.

TrainingAIController supplies movement-only training behavior.

Keep this architecture.

Do not put CPU decision logic directly into Fighter, CombatSystem, BattleScreen, or character data.

Create a separate combat-capable controller, preferably named something like:

CombatAIController

or another clear equivalent.

Keep TrainingAIController for Practice Ground.

Quick Battle should use the new combat AI controller.

The CPU must interact with the game by generating the same input fields a human controller generates:

left
right
charge
jump
defense
primary
special
action1
action2

and their appropriate ...Pressed edge values.

Do not have the AI call attacks, damage functions, techniques, Shield methods, projectile spawners, movement methods, etc. directly.

A CPU attack must happen because the controller pressed the same action that a human would press.

A CPU Shield must happen because defense was held.

A charged action must happen through the existing Charge + action interaction.

A Dash must use the existing dash input semantics rather than directly changing velocity.

This principle from Seren is essential:

Difficulty changes how well the CPU thinks, not what its fighter is allowed to do.

