8. Difficulty profiles

Create one centralized configuration describing CPU capability at each level.

Take conceptual inspiration from Seren’s DIFFS, where difficulty independently controls things such as reaction time, thinking frequency, lapse probability, planning depth and decision noise.

For Alva, use a fighting-game-appropriate configuration along these lines:

Trait	Easy	Medium	Hard	Brutal
Reaction delay	high	moderate	low	very low
Reassessment interval	slow	normal	fast	very fast
Mistake/lapse chance	high	moderate	low	very low
Decision randomness	high	moderate	low	minimal
Defensive awareness	low	moderate	high	very high
Punish awareness	low	moderate	high	very high
Charged-action judgment	rare/simple	situational	strong	very strong
Dash usage	rare/none	occasional	deliberate	deliberate
Planning persistence	none/short	short	medium	medium/high
Threat prediction	current danger	limited	good	strong

Put concrete numeric values behind these traits.

A reasonable starting point is approximately:

Easy reaction window: 0.45–0.85 s
Medium: 0.22–0.45 s
Hard: 0.10–0.24 s
Brutal: 0.05–0.14 s

Do not treat those exact values as sacred. Tune them against Alva’s actual attack startup times and simulation speed so Easy is visibly forgiving while Brutal is demanding but still physically believable.

The important property is monotonic behavior:

Easy should never systematically have better reactions or more accurate judgment than Medium.

Medium should not outperform Hard by configuration.

Hard should not have better decision parameters than Brutal.

Randomness may create individual moments where a lower difficulty makes a good choice, which is desirable, but the underlying capability should clearly increase.

9. AI architecture: Sense → Evaluate → Act

Follow Seren’s useful separation between sensing, weighing choices and acting.

The combat AI should not select a totally new random action every simulation frame.

It should maintain internal state such as:

a think/reassessment timer,
reaction timer(s),
current movement intention,
short-term plan,
plan expiration,
held inputs,
queued one-frame input edges,
charge intention,
defensive intention,
dash tap state,
and injected RNG.

On each relevant decision cycle:

Sense

Build a snapshot from information available in the simulation.

Useful information includes:

the CPU fighter’s position and velocity,
grounded/airborne state,
current surface,
facing,
energy,
Shield exhaustion,
Launch Point,
current attack/recovery/stun state,
charged-action cooldowns,
opponent position and velocity,
opponent grounded/airborne state,
opponent attack phase,
opponent Shield state,
opponent Charge state,
opponent technique state,
relative horizontal and vertical distance,
ledge/void danger,
platform geometry,
live enemy projectiles,
live summons/clones,
battle score,
time remaining,
and whether either fighter is temporarily out because of the Void.

Use ctx.stage and ctx.battle rather than introducing global game state.

Evaluate

Score reasonable candidate intentions according to the situation and difficulty.

Examples include:

hold position,
approach,
retreat,
jump,
drop through a one-way platform,
dash forward,
dash away,
Shield,
ground BA1,
ground BA2,
air BA1,
air BA2,
Throw/projectile,
Charge,
charged BA1,
charged BA2,
or wait/bait.

Do not assume every fighter supports every candidate. Derive legal actions from that fighter’s data.

Act

Translate the selected intention into normal FighterInput.

The controller decides.

The existing fighter/combat engine executes.

10. Reaction behavior

Difficulty must affect actual reaction behavior, not just attack frequency.

Do not let every CPU notice a player startup or projectile on the same simulation frame.

When a new threat is perceived, response should be delayed according to the selected difficulty.

Easy should often see an attack too late or fail to respond at all.

Medium should react to obvious, slower threats but still get caught.

Hard should respond reliably to telegraphed attacks and obvious projectiles.

Brutal should react rapidly and make strong choices, but should still have a small human-like response delay.

Do NOT give Brutal literal zero-frame reactions.

Do NOT read the player’s raw InputManager state to know what button they just pressed before the fighter expresses it in the simulation.

The CPU may observe things the fighter itself makes visible to the simulation, such as an opponent entering attack startup, moving, jumping, Charging, Shielding, beginning a technique or releasing a projectile.

That is fair information.

11. No cheating

Difficulty may NEVER directly alter:

fighter damage,
Base Launch,
Directional Launch,
hitstun,
attack startup,
attack recovery,
movement speed,
gravity,
jump speed,
Dash speed,
Dash cost,
Shield cost,
maximum Energy,
Energy recovery,
cooldowns,
invulnerability,
hitboxes,
hurtboxes,
score,
respawn time,
or any other core fighter mechanic.

There must not be logic such as:

if brutal -> damage *= 1.2

or:

if easy -> CPU fighter speed *= 0.8

The fighter is mechanically identical regardless of difficulty.

Only the controller gets smarter/faster.

Likewise, the AI must not teleport, cancel recovery illegally, ignore stun, attack during states where a human cannot, create projectiles directly, or bypass charged-action cooldowns.

12. Neutral movement and stage awareness

Preserve and reuse the good stage-following ideas already present in TrainingAIController, but do not tightly couple the two controllers if that makes the training behavior fragile.

The combat CPU needs to:

follow the opponent across platforms,
navigate toward useful fighting distance,
jump to higher platforms when appropriate,
drop through one-way platforms when appropriate,
avoid casually walking straight into the Void,
stop or turn before unsafe ledges,
recover its positioning after being separated,
and stand still while its opponent is currently lost to the Void and awaiting respawn.

It should understand vertical separation instead of endlessly running into the platform underneath the player.

Higher difficulty should make better navigation decisions and commit fewer positioning mistakes, but every level must remain functional.

13. Fighting-distance logic

The CPU needs meaningful spacing.

Do not implement combat as:

“if opponent exists, hold toward them and mash BA1.”

Use the actual fighter attack data where practical.

At close range the CPU can consider BA1/BA2, Shield, retreating, jumping or a charged option.

At medium range it can approach, bait, Throw, reposition or prepare Charge.

At long range it should close distance intelligently or use available ranged tools.

Where practical, calculate approximate attack reach from the character’s resolved attack hitbox rather than hard-coding #0001-specific pixel distances.

Account for whether an action uses a different ground or air attack.

14. Offensive decision-making

The CPU must actually fight at every difficulty.

Easy should still attack. It is inexperienced, not disabled.

Suggested feel:

Easy: approaches imperfectly, pauses often, attacks at obvious opportunities, sometimes uses the wrong move for the spacing, rarely uses advanced charged actions, misses punish windows, has substantial decision noise.

Medium: gives a normal competent fight, uses both basic attacks, Throw, occasional Shield and Charge, understands basic spacing, sometimes uses charged actions and sometimes makes mistakes.

Hard: actively controls spacing, punishes obvious recovery, uses projectiles and air attacks appropriately, Shields real threats, uses Charge and charged actions deliberately, understands stage position, and makes fewer wasted inputs.

Brutal: reassesses quickly, capitalizes on recovery and bad positioning, makes strong attack/defense choices, uses the full moveset intelligently, manages charged cooldowns and Energy well, pressures without simply holding forward, and rarely throws away a safe advantage.

Do not turn Brutal into constant aggression. Sometimes the strongest choice is waiting, spacing, Shielding, retreating, or Charging.

15. Defense and threat assessment

The CPU should understand the existing Shield mechanic.

Shield is a held Defense input, costs Energy only when it blocks a hit, and becomes unavailable while Energy-exhausted.

The AI should therefore consider:

whether a threat can actually reach it,
how soon the threat becomes active,
whether Shield is currently legal,
whether its Energy can support blocking,
whether moving/jumping away is better,
and whether the opponent is simply whiffing outside range.

Easy should Shield infrequently and often late.

Medium should block obvious threats some of the time.

Hard should make solid defensive choices.

Brutal should react quickly and intelligently but not hold Shield permanently.

A Brutal CPU that simply turtles forever is not a successful implementation.

16. Projectiles and Throw

#0001’s primary input is Throw and creates the shuriken through the normal attack/projectile pipeline.

The CPU should understand projectiles on both offense and defense.

On offense, Throw is a useful ranged option when spacing supports it.

On defense, harder difficulties should notice enemy projectiles earlier and choose between Shielding, repositioning or another legal response.

Easy may simply get hit by many projectiles.

Do not despawn, redirect or otherwise manipulate projectiles from the controller.

17. Charge and charged actions

The CPU must be capable of using Alva’s actual Charge system.

Remember the existing semantics:

Charge is held.
The fighter must already have been Charging before a charged action press can trigger the charged version.
Charged actions have their own cooldowns.
A charged action may be a summon or a full charged technique.
If an action cannot perform its charged form for the normal gameplay reasons, existing Fighter behavior decides the fallback.

Therefore the AI needs stateful input across multiple simulation steps.

It cannot emit charge + actionPressed for one isolated frame and assume a charged move happens.

It should be able to:

enter Charge when there is enough space/time,
remain in Charge long enough to qualify,
select an available charged action,
respect cooldowns,
release Charge when the situation becomes unsafe,
and avoid repeatedly pressing a charged action that is unavailable.

For #0001 specifically this means the CPU can eventually make intelligent use of both the Clone Attack and Sphere Rush through the existing character data.

Easy should rarely execute them well.

Medium may use them in obvious situations.

Hard and Brutal should use them deliberately.

Keep the implementation generic enough for future characters with different charged actions.

18. Dash

Alva’s Dash is triggered through horizontal double-tap semantics and spends Energy.

The combat AI may use Dash, especially on Hard and Brutal, but it must do so through the existing input path.

Do not set velocity directly.

The AI should understand that Dash competes with attacks and Energy/Shield needs.

Easy can use it rarely or not at all.

Medium may occasionally Dash to close a clear gap.

Hard and Brutal can use it intentionally for positioning, approach or escape.

19. Punishing and recovery awareness

Higher difficulty should understand when an opponent has committed to an action.

Do this using existing observable combat state such as attack phase/recovery rather than reading future input.

Easy largely ignores punish windows.

Medium occasionally notices an obvious whiff.

Hard should punish significant recovery when properly positioned.

Brutal should capitalize quickly on clear whiffs and recovery while still respecting range and stage safety.

Do not create animation cancels that the normal fighter cannot perform.

20. Match awareness

The AI may use Quick Battle state as part of its strategy.

Quick Battle is a scored match with Void points, respawns and a timer.

Use ctx.battle when useful.

Examples:

A CPU leading near the end of the timer may value stage safety slightly more.

A CPU behind may take slightly more offensive risk.

A fighter with a dangerous Launch Point near a ledge should value center-stage recovery.

A high-difficulty CPU should understand that knocking the player toward the Void is strategically useful.

Keep these effects restrained. Difficulty should not create bizarre personality changes every second.

21. Fair perception and limited prediction

Hard and Brutal may project short-term motion from current position/velocity and visible attack state.

For example, they may estimate where a projectile or opponent will be a short time into the future.

The prediction horizon should increase with difficulty.

That is acceptable.

They may NOT inspect future inputs, queued browser events, hidden player intentions or anything outside the game state a fighter could conceptually react to.

Brutal should feel observant, not psychic.

22. Randomness and reproducibility

Follow the existing pattern of injecting RNG into controllers.

The AI should accept an RNG function so tests can be deterministic.

Battle can continue creating a seeded RNG through the existing mulberry32 utility.

Do not scatter direct Math.random() calls throughout the combat AI if they make deterministic testing impossible.

Difficulty should control decision noise and lapse probability.

Randomness exists to make fights less robotic, not to replace decision-making.

23. Input-edge correctness

Be extremely careful with ...Pressed.

A one-frame press should only be true on the transition/decision frame where the CPU intends to press the action.

Do not leave:

action1Pressed
action2Pressed
primaryPressed
jumpPressed
etc.

stuck true across frames.

Held actions such as movement, Defense and Charge should remain held only for the intended duration.

The CPU controller must output complete, predictable input snapshots consistent with the format expected by Fighter.update().

